<HelpChapterSection Id="account">
    <HelpTopicSection Id="account-first-login">
        <p>There is no self-registration in KOD: accounts are created by the HR department or the system administrator. They give you an <strong>email address</strong> (your login) and a <strong>temporary password</strong>. The first sign-in has three parts and takes 3–5 minutes.</p>
        <p>Have a smartphone ready with an authenticator app: <strong>Google Authenticator</strong>, <strong>Microsoft Authenticator</strong> or any other app that supports TOTP one-time codes (for example, 1Password, Bitwarden, Authy).</p>
        <h4>Part 1. The password from the administrator</h4>
        <ol class="help-steps">
            <li>Open the sign-in page and enter your <span class="help-ui">Email</span> and <span class="help-ui">Password</span> — the temporary password you were given.</li>
            <li>Click <span class="help-ui">Continue</span>.</li>
        </ol>
        <h4>Part 2. Your own password (“Step 1 of 2”)</h4>
        <ol class="help-steps">
            <li>The system opens the <span class="help-ui">Create your own password</span> page. The temporary password is valid for the first sign-in only.</li>
            <li>Enter a <span class="help-ui">New password</span> and <span class="help-ui">Repeat password</span>. Requirements: 12 to 128 characters, uppercase and lowercase letters and digits, at least 5 different characters. The new password must differ from the temporary one.</li>
            <li>Click <span class="help-ui">Save password</span>. If the password appears in known data breaches, the system asks you to choose another one — this protects you from guessing attacks based on “popular” passwords.</li>
        </ol>
        <h4>Part 3. The authenticator (“Step 2 of 2”)</h4>
        <ol class="help-steps">
            <li>On the <span class="help-ui">Protect your sign-in</span> page, open the authenticator app on your phone and add a new account.</li>
            <li>Scan the QR code with your phone camera. If scanning is inconvenient, choose manual entry in the app and type the key shown under the QR code (the <span class="help-ui">Copy</span> button copies it).</li>
            <li>A “KOD” entry with a 6-digit code that changes every 30 seconds appears in the app. Enter the current code in the <span class="help-ui">Code from the app</span> field and click <span class="help-ui">Confirm and sign in</span>.</li>
            <li>The system shows <strong>10 recovery codes</strong>. Click <span class="help-ui">Copy codes</span> and keep them in a safe place (a password manager, a printed sheet in a safe). They will not be shown again.</li>
            <li>Click <span class="help-ui">Go to the cabinet</span>.</li>
        </ol>
        <h4>Part 4. Profile photo</h4>
        <p>The cabinet opens the <span class="help-ui">Add a profile photo</span> page: choose a photo, place the face inside the circle and click <span class="help-ui">Save photo</span> (see “<a href="/help#profile-photo">Profile photo</a>”). The other sections are unavailable without a photo.</p>
        <Alert Tone="Tone.Outline" Icon="info" Title="Why this is mandatory">Two-factor authentication is mandatory for all accounts, including administrators. Even if someone learns your password, they cannot sign in without your phone.</Alert>
        <p>If you don't finish setting up the authenticator within 10 minutes, the page returns you to the sign-in page — just sign in again, this time with your own password.</p>
    </HelpTopicSection>

    <HelpTopicSection Id="account-sign-in">
        <ol class="help-steps">
            <li>Enter your <span class="help-ui">Email</span> and <span class="help-ui">Password</span>.</li>
            <li>If you wish, check <span class="help-ui">Remember me on this device</span> — the session is then kept after the browser is closed. Don't do this on shared computers.</li>
            <li>Click <span class="help-ui">Continue</span>.</li>
            <li>On the <span class="help-ui">Authenticator code</span> page, enter the 6-digit code from the app in the <span class="help-ui">Verification code</span> field and click <span class="help-ui">Sign in</span>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Each code can be used <strong>only once</strong>. If a code has just been used (for example, you signed out and are signing in again right away), wait for the next code in the app — the system will say “The code is incorrect or no longer valid. Try the next code.”</p>
        <p>If codes are not accepted, make sure automatic time is turned on on your phone: the codes depend on the clock, and the allowed drift is up to 30 seconds.</p>
        <p>After signing in you return to the page that redirected you to sign in, or to <span class="help-ui">Home</span>.</p>
        <p>To change the interface language before signing in, use the language button in the top-right corner of the sign-in page. After you sign in, the language saved in your profile applies (see “<a href="/help#profile-overview">My profile</a>”).</p>
    </HelpTopicSection>

    <HelpTopicSection Id="account-recovery">
        <p>If you don't have your phone at hand, sign in with one of the recovery codes you received when setting up the authenticator.</p>
        <ol class="help-steps">
            <li>Enter your email and password as usual.</li>
            <li>On the code page, click <span class="help-ui">Sign in with a recovery code</span>.</li>
            <li>Enter the code in the <span class="help-ui">Recovery code</span> field (format <code class="ds-code">XXXXX-XXXXX</code>) and click <span class="help-ui">Sign in</span>.</li>
            <li>Each recovery code works once. After signing in, generate new codes in your profile (see “<a href="/help#profile-security">Security: password, 2FA, recovery codes</a>”).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>A sign-in with a recovery code is not remembered after the browser is closed.</p>
    </HelpTopicSection>

    <HelpTopicSection Id="account-lockout">
        <p>After <strong>5 failed attempts</strong> in a row (a wrong password, authenticator code or recovery code), sign-in to the account is temporarily blocked for <strong>15 minutes</strong>. A wrong current password when changing your password and wrong verification codes in the profile also count towards the limit.</p>
        <p>For security reasons, the sign-in page gives the same answer to any error: “Incorrect email or password. After several failed attempts, sign-in is temporarily blocked.” — so outsiders cannot find out whether an account exists. If you are sure of your password but cannot sign in, wait 15 minutes.</p>
        <p>If your account has been <strong>blocked by an administrator</strong>, after entering the correct password you will see “The account is blocked. Contact your administrator.”</p>
        <p>An administrator can lift a temporary lockout earlier with the <span class="help-ui">End all sessions</span> button on the user's page.</p>
    </HelpTopicSection>

    <HelpTopicSection Id="account-forgot">
        <p>KOD has no password recovery by email — this way nobody can take over your access through your mailbox.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Forgot your password</strong> — contact the HR department or the system administrator. You will get a new temporary password; when you sign in, the system asks you to replace it with your own.</li>
            <li><strong>Changed or lost your phone</strong> — sign in with a recovery code and ask the administrator to reset your authenticator. You will set up the new phone the next time you sign in. If you have no recovery codes, the administrator resets the authenticator and you set up a new one when you sign in.</li>
            <li><strong>Suspect someone else knows your password</strong> — change your password immediately (all other sessions will be ended) and check the list of sessions in your profile.</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpTopicSection>

    <HelpTopicSection Id="account-change-password">
        <ol class="help-steps">
            <li>Open <span class="help-ui">Profile</span> → the <span class="help-ui">Security</span> tab → <span class="help-ui">Change password</span>.</li>
            <li>Enter your <span class="help-ui">Current password</span>, <span class="help-ui">New password</span> and <span class="help-ui">Repeat new password</span>.</li>
            <li>Click <span class="help-ui">Save password</span>. The message “Password changed” appears.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>After the password is changed, <strong>all other sessions are ended</strong> — you will need to sign in again on other devices. The current session keeps working.</p>
        <p>If an administrator gave you a temporary password and your authenticator is already set up, after signing in with a code the system immediately opens the password change page: “You signed in with a temporary password. Set your own password to continue.” Other pages are unavailable until you change the password.</p>
    </HelpTopicSection>

    <HelpTopicSection Id="account-sessions">
        <p>A <strong>session</strong> is a sign-in from a particular browser on a particular device. Each session ends automatically:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>after <strong>8 hours of inactivity</strong>;</li>
            <li>no later than <strong>12 hours after sign-in</strong>, even if you have been working all the time — then just sign in again.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>To sign out, click the sign-out button <Icon Name="log-out" /> next to your name at the bottom of the side menu (on a phone — in the <Icon Name="menu" /> menu) and confirm “Sign out of the cabinet?”.</p>
        <p>The list of all your sessions is in <span class="help-ui">Profile</span> → <span class="help-ui">Sessions</span> (see “<a href="/help#profile-sessions">Active sessions</a>”). An ended session stops working immediately, even if a page is still open on that device.</p>
        <p>Sessions are also ended when an administrator blocks the account, issues a temporary password or resets the authenticator.</p>
    </HelpTopicSection>

    <HelpTopicSection Id="account-security-tips">
        <ul>
            <li>Don't use your KOD password for other services. A password manager is the most convenient way to store passwords.</li>
            <li>Never share authenticator codes or recovery codes with anyone — KOD staff never ask for them.</li>
            <li>On shared computers, don't check “Remember me” and always sign out when you finish.</li>
            <li>Review your list of sessions regularly and end the ones you don't recognize.</li>
            <li>The private key of your electronic signature and its password are separate from your KOD account; they are never sent to the server (see the “<a href="/help#signing">Signing and verifying files</a>” chapter).</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpTopicSection>
</HelpChapterSection>
